Number Match
The aim of the game is to collect pairs of cards that show the same number.&nbsp;
Place the cards blank side up on the table.
Take it in turns to turn over two cards.&nbsp;
If the two cards have the same number, keep them.
If they have different numbers, turn them back over.&nbsp;
The game ends when there are no cards left.&nbsp;
Repeat the game to practise matching numbers.
You will need:
A set of number cards (if you do not have any printed, you can make your own)
875926735How to play:

Teddy Bear Picnic
How to play:
Ask your child to share the plates out equally between the teddies.&nbsp;
Repeat this for each other item. Make sure that each teddy has one of each item.&nbsp;
925413995Getting ready:
Set out about four teddies (you could also use dolls or action figures).&nbsp;
Gather a pile of plates, forks, knives and spoons to share between them. Alternatively, you could use food such as grapes, sweets and apples.

Hello Starfish
875923974Playdough RecipeHow to play:
The aim of the activity is to make a starfish with five legs.&nbsp;
With your child, role out five legs and a body using the dough.
Challenge your child to attach the legs to the body one at a time, counting as they do so.&nbsp;
After they have made the starfish, ask them to count how many legs it has.&nbsp;
You will need:
FPlay dough, plasticine or blue tack. You could make your own play dough using the recipe below if you've got time.

Pin the legs on the insect
879361577
Go outside and ask your child to collect six sticks. Or collect six pencils or pens indoors. These will be the legs for the insect.&nbsp;
Ask them to place the sticks around one of the insects to make its legs.&nbsp;
Repeat for the other two insects.&nbsp;
How to play:
You will need:
Three insect body shapes.&nbsp;
You can cut out the bugs on the template below or make your own.

Finding Threes
875921077
Place the cards blank side up on the table.
Ask your child to turn over the cards one at a time.&nbsp;
If the card has exactly three dots, your child gets to keep it (ask them to count the dots to show you that they're sure).&nbsp;
If there aren't exactly three dots, you keep it.&nbsp;
Play until all the cards have been turned over.
You will need:
A set of dot cards (you can download below or copy the images with a pen onto paper). Cut or tear carefully along the lines.
How to play:
The aim of the game is to find all of the cards which show exactly three dots.&nbsp;

Numbers in 9
How to play:
You will need:
Ask your child to fetch nine small toys, such as building blocks or model animals.
879356774
Divide the toys into two groups.&nbsp;
Ask your child to count how many are in each group, then count how many there are overall.
Put the toys back together.&nbsp;
Then, ask your child to split them into two piles again, this time with a different number in each pile. For example, if the first time there was one pile of six and one pile of three, this time, you could have one pile of five and one pile of four.&nbsp;
Ask your child to count the number of toys in each group again. Then, ask them to count how many toys there are altogether.&nbsp;
Repeat the activity to practise different numbers within nine.&nbsp;

10 Green Bottles
Ten green bottles lyrics879368591How to play:
879368113
Line up the bottles.
Help your child to sing the song.&nbsp;
At the end of each verse, ask your child to knock a bottle over.&nbsp;
By the end of the song, there should be no bottles left.&nbsp;
Repeat the activity to help your child learn the song!
You will need:
Ten empty bottles. These could be plastic bottles, reusable bottles, empty shampoo bottles or empty sauce bottles.
Challenge:
Use your ten bottles to play a game of skittles.
Set the skittles up together.&nbsp;
Then, ask your child to roll or throw the ball at the skittles.&nbsp;
Ask them to count how many they have knocked over. Then, ask them to count how many are still standing. Finally, ask them to count how many bottles there are overall.&nbsp;&nbsp;
Pick the bottles back up and repeat the activity, taking it in turns with your child to throw the ball.&nbsp;
Play as many times as you like to practise counting numbers within ten!&nbsp;

Snake Size
How to play:
Playdough recipeYou will need:
Blue tack, plasticine or play dough. You can make your own play dough using the recipe below.&nbsp;
Ask your child to make a small snake by rolling out some of the play dough.&nbsp;
Then, ask them to make another snake, longer than the first.&nbsp;
Next, challenge them to make a shorter snake than the first one.&nbsp;
Challenge your child to make different sizes of snake to practise comparing amounts!
879365599

It's Pouring
875926787Let your child experiment pouring water between the cups.
How many of the smallest cup does it take to fill the biggest cup?&nbsp;
How many of the biggest cup does it take to fill the smallest cup?&nbsp;
How many small cups does it take to fill a medium cup?&nbsp;
Can your child order the cups from biggest to smallest?
You will need:
Around 5 plastic cups and jugs, all different sizes.&nbsp;
A washing up bowl or bucket filled with water.
How to play:

Two truths one lie
Extra Challenge:
This graph shows the average maximum temperatures in Lisbon and Madrid.
Can you make up two true statements and one false statement to go with it?
See if your family can find the false statement!&nbsp;
You'll need:
The graphs
If you haven't been given one, you can download or print a copy below.
A graph showing the monthly average temperatures of Lisbon and Madrid.What to do:
Look carefully at the first graph.
One of these statements is incorrect. Can you work out which one?&nbsp;
The average temperature for every month is higher in England than in Scotland.&nbsp;
On average, it is 4°C hotter in England than Scotland in June.&nbsp;&nbsp;
The average temperature in Scotland in April is the same as the average temperature in England in October.&nbsp;
A graph showing the monthly average temperatures in England and Scotland.

Character dressing up
763702720Dressing up clothes ideasIn this activity, you are going to pretend to be characters from a story. Pretending to be characters helps children think about the story in more depth, particularly about the characters, their feelings, and why they acted the way they did.How to play:
Choose a character each from a story. You could be from the same story or different stories, either is fun (though the same story is easier to start with).
Dress up as your characters (dressing up clothes ideas below).
Play together as your characters. This could be:
Acting out part of the story together;
Pretend to go on an adventure together in the garden;
Build a den together;
Have a pretend tea party;
Do an ordinary household activity but as your characters, e.g. setting the table for dinner.
Let your child take the lead and see where they take you!

Would you rather
Question suggestions:
764503817How to play:
Discuss one of the 'would you rather' questions together.&nbsp;
More question suggestionsThere is no "right" answer to these questions, so your discussion should last several minutes, as you try to work out what you think, explain your reasons, and work out what might make you choose the other option. Both people should share their ideas!&nbsp;

The human robot
763799394Ideas of everyday tasks:
Putting on a hat, scarf and gloves;
Making a sandwich;
Running the bath;
Putting on your shoes;
Making a cup of tea.
How to play:
You are going to be a robot and your child is going to program you to carry out an everyday task. They will give you instructions and you should follow them exactly. The instructions may not work! Your child might need to change what they are saying to help you to complete the task!

Tell me a story
764514421The activity:
Work together to retell a story you know.
Ideas:
Here are some ways you could retell the story:
Say the words, taking it in turns to add the next part of the story;
Use mini-figures to act out the story;
Make little puppets to help you;
Use the pictures in a book but use your own words;
Some children's books have a story map in the back to use for retelling stories;
Draw your own story map (see example above).

The story bag
For this activity, you will need a story bag. It can be any bag you have in the house, though it's better if it's not see-through!
764126139The activity:
When you have filled your bag, the storytelling can begin!
You can use the bag in any way you like, but here are some ideas you could choose from:
Choose an object to be the main character in your story;
Start telling a story and pull out an object to help you decide what happens next (like in the video below);
You could take out all the objects at once and use them to set up a story scene;
Retell a story you know, but swap the objects in the story for things in the bag and see what happens;
Choose three things from the bag and create a story which uses all three.
Filling Your bagCloth bag filled with small household items

Fruit Kebabs
What to do:
Ask your child to place one piece of each different fruit onto the skewer, or in a line on a plate, in any order they choose.&nbsp;
Challenge your child to repeat the pattern until you have used all of the fruit. &lt;
Ask your child to describe the pattern to you as you go along, for example: banana, grape, apple, banana, grape, apple...
769468189You will need:
Three types of fruit cut up into pieces.
Some kebab skewers or a surface to place the fruit on.&nbsp;
If you don't have fruit, you could use three different items such as pasta pieces, pebbles and buttons.
Safety reminder: Whole grapes can be a choking hazard - cut grapes in half lengthwise before eating.

Steps
766857180How to play:
Ask your child to estimate how many steps it will take them to get from A to B across the room. For example, how many steps from the sofa to the radiator?&nbsp;
How close was their estimate to the real answer? Were you higher or lower?&nbsp;
Now challenge your child to estimate how many steps it will take you to walk the same distance from A to B. Ask them to compare it to how many steps they did.&nbsp;

Shopping bag
This is a perfect activity to do after you've been to the supermarket or you can simply fill up a bag with groceries.770678578How to play:
Spread out the things from your bag of shopping.&nbsp;
Ask your child to describe the shape and size of each item.&nbsp;
Which shapes stack?&nbsp;
Which shapes roll?&nbsp;
Which are rigid?&nbsp;
Which will get squashed?&nbsp;
Which ones need to go on top and why?&nbsp;
Pack the shopping bag together. Talk about how to make everything fit without getting anything squashed!

Teddy's coins
Keeping ourselves safeYou will need:
Two teddies
Five or six coins
How to play:
Share the coins between the teddies (they don't need to have the same number) and ask your child how many coins each teddy has.&nbsp;
Now move all the coins from one teddy and give them to the other.&nbsp;
How many coins does each teddy have now?&nbsp;
Repeat the activity to practise the number zero.
769410767

Stopwatch
769532045How to play:
Ask your child to do an action such as stand on one leg or hopping on the spot.&nbsp;
Use the stopwatch below (or the one on your phone) to time how long they did it for.&nbsp;
Ask them how long they think it was and compare it with the actual time.&nbsp;
Can they guess how long it will take them to run round the room or do another activity?

The Alphabet Song
Sing the alphabet song together!
We have included two versions of the song for you to try. This first one might be a new tune to you, but it is really helpful because it helps you say all the letters separately.&nbsp;
Try to encourage the British pronunciation of z 'zed' rather than the American z 'zee'.&nbsp;
Children often get muddled in the middle of the alphabet with the letters l m n o p. Try to slow down their singing of this part (like in this second video) so they really sound like separate letters and not one "lemonenopea"!
762323131863526790Which version of the song did you prefer?

Why in Stories
What to Do:
While you are reading, stop and ask some of these questions (maybe just choose one at a time, but cover several as you go through the whole story):
Why did she do that?
Why is he sad/happy/angry/excited?
Why do you think they used the word ‘…’ there?
Why is that happening in the picture?
Did you like the book? Why did/didn’t you like this book?
Find a story to read together, either a real book or one of these suggestions:
A book from home, school or the library;
A YouTube reading of a story;
A story on CBeebies Radio;
One of the CBeebies bedtime stories;
An audiobook from the library;
A story you can tell without a book.

How do I brush my teeth?
Talk together about how you brush your teeth. What things do you need to remember to do every time?The Activity:
Write instructions for how to brush your teeth. Make sure you:
Put numbers for each instruction;
Write the instructions in the order you need to do them;
Include all the things you need to do;
Use capital letters to start sentences and full stops to end them.
Need Help?
Have a look at these example instructions:
Example Instructions

Making Breakfast
Activity:
Write instructions for how to make breakfast (or you could choose another meal).
You must:
Think about what to do before you start;
Include at least five steps;
Use a number to start each step;
Use an adverb at the start of each step to help make them easier to follow. You can choose from the list below or come up with your own;
Try not to use the same adverb more than once;
If you are feeling fancy, see if you can use a comma after the adverb, e.g. ‘First, put a plate on the table.’
Adverbs You Could Use:
first, then, next, slowly, carefully, finally
Example Instructions with Adverbs

Bedtime story
Find somewhere comfy to sit and listen to a story! Reading a real book is good because it is easy to stop, look at the pictures and talk about what is happening. But for this activity, you could use a story in another format. Here are some suggestions:
A book from home, school or the library;
A YouTube reading of a story;
A story on CBeebies Radio;
An audiobook from the library;
Tell a story you know without using a book.
Top Tips:
Make sure you can both see/hear the story so you can both listen and talk about it.
Let your child turn the pages (if you’re using a book) – this lets them really look at the pictures and move through the story at their own pace.
Encourage them to talk about the pictures – you can ask if they can see something specific in the picture, or let them tell you about it.
When you have finished the story, you can ask them if they liked it and if they had a favourite character.
Other Ways to Explore a Story915160456

What Mark will it Make?
847135798You Will Need:
A collection of about ten objects you can use to make marks (and that you don’t mind getting a bit messy);
Something to make marks (paint, flour, ketchup, water);
Something to make marks on (paper, a cereal box, a tray).
How to play:
Dip the objects in your paint/flour/ketchup/water.
Use them to make marks on your paper/tray/card.
What marks do they make?
Can you use one thing to make different kinds of marks?&nbsp;

Snails!
In this activity, you are going to draw lots of snails. Make sure your child is drawing anti-clockwise spirals (this will get them ready for writing letters).
You could draw your snails:
On paper using coloured pencils;
Using paint, at a table or an easel;
Using a stick in the mud outside;
Using a water pistol on an outdoor wall;
With your finger in a tray of sand or flour;
With chalk on the garden path.
915124504You snails can just be spirals, or you can add in their bodies after you’ve drawn the shells!

Say a Sentence
A koala with an idea lightbulb above its head.There are four types of sentences in this activity:
{{definition "Statement" "A statement sentence is the most common type of sentence. These tell the person reading a fact or idea about a single topic."}}&nbsp;
{{definition "Question" "A question sentence is used to ask something, usually to find out information."}}&nbsp;
{{definition "Exclamation" "An exclamation sentence begins with 'what' or 'how', includes a verb and ends with an exclamation mark. For instance, ‘What big eyes you have!’ and ‘How brilliant you were at swimming!’ are both exclamations sentences."}}&nbsp;
{{definition "Command" "A command sentence tells someone to do something. They often start with a verb, e.g. 'Brush your hair.'"}}&nbsp;
&nbsp;
How to Play:
Roll the die below.
Say a sentence which matches the word on the die.
Repeat until you have done a few of each sentence type.

One more and one less cards
910816789You will need:
Two sets of 0-10 cards.&nbsp;
Make sure they are separated into the two sets (digits 0-10 and ten frames 0-10).
Player 1 chooses a digit card and puts it on the table (or floor).
Player 2 finds the digit which is one more and puts it to the right of the first card.
Player 2 finds the digit which is one less and puts it to the left of the first card.&nbsp;
Player 1 finds the three tens frames that match the digits and puts them under the digit cards.&nbsp;
Swap over and play again from step 1!
