Beat the clock
What next?Here are some questions you could ask while you are playing. Questions are a great way to help your child with their mathematical thinking and reasoning.
&nbsp;
How to play:&nbsp;
&nbsp;Choose an action (e.g. star jumps, hops, claps, write your name, or draw a triangle).&nbsp;
Start the timer.&nbsp;
Count how many times you can do the action in the time.&nbsp;
You will need:
A ten second timer. You could use the timer on your phone, our timer, or have a look at our other ideas for timers.&nbsp;
Other ideas for timers

Pencil race
This activity helps children practise the correct grip for holding a pencil.
Crab claws warmup:&nbsp;
Practise pinching your finger and thumb together like they are crab claws. Your hand makes a similar shape as when we hold a pencil. Can you walk around like a crab?
A crabKeeping ourselves safe
This game helps us practise our pencil grip, but remember pencils aren't a toy - they are sharp and we need to use them carefully. Talk about keeping them away from our eyes and other people.Pencil race:
Get a pencil or pen. Practise holding it correctly:&nbsp;
1. Pinch the end of the pencil in between your finger and thumb.&nbsp;
2. Flip the pencil over so it's resting on your hand.
3. Grip the pencil ready to write.&nbsp;&nbsp;
Can you practise doing this as fast as you can?&nbsp;
Have a race, who can pick up their pencil fastest when you say "go"?&nbsp;

Find me something...
How to play:
1. Choose a descriptive word and ask:&nbsp;
Can you find me something _______?
A child holding and feeling a pine cone.You can use the examples below if you like:&nbsp;
2. See if your partner can find an object nearby that matches that description. &nbsp;
3. When they find an object, ask:&nbsp;
What other words describe this object?

Fibber
The aim of the game:&nbsp;
Tell a true story containing one lie. Can the other person spot the fib?
A Mathscot is thinking.What kind of story should I tell?
It can be anything, as long as it is something that really happened! Here are some ideas:
Talk about something you did at school;
Talk about a trip you went on;
Tell a true story from history;
Talk about something that happened in the news;
Tell the other person something they did when they were a baby.
&nbsp;

What is it?
How to play:
There are several words below. For each word, there are four sentences.&nbsp;
Read each sentence aloud.
Work together to decide whether the word in that sentence is a noun, a verb, an adjective or an adverb.&nbsp;
Explain how you knew.

When the rain came down
What to do:
Work together to create a weather poem by adding a main clause in the space for each of the sentences below.
What words should we choose?You can say your poem aloud or you can write it down if you prefer!&nbsp;
Lightning in a dark sky.Weather Poem
When the rain came down, _______________________.
If it had been sunny, _______________________.&nbsp;
Although it had not yet snowed, _______________________.&nbsp;
Because the storm had begun, _______________________.&nbsp;

Can you find half?
What to do
Choose an object and ask your child how they could find half.&nbsp;
Have a go at splitting it in half together.
Ideas for objects
A piece of paper, a leaf, a banana, some wool, a handful of pasta, a ball of playdoh, a slice of bread, a cup of water.&nbsp;
Questions to ask
Is this exactly half or roughly half?&nbsp;&nbsp;
How can we check we have half?
Which things are easier or harder to find half of?
Extra Challenge
A quarter is half of a half.&nbsp;
Can you find quarters?

Clap! Clap! Clap!
Challenge:
You could try the same activity using a shaker or a musical instrument! You could even make your own shaker by putting small objects inside a box or bottle.&nbsp;
Things to think about:
How many claps you do;
How fast or slow the claps are;
The spaces between the claps;
The rhythm you use;
How loud or quiet the claps are;
Whether the pattern is the same all the way through or changes.&nbsp;
How to make a shakerHow to play:
Take it in turns to clap a pattern for the other person to copy.
Start with some simple patterns (e.g. clap, clap, clap) where there isn’t too much to think about, and then move onto some more complicated patterns.&nbsp;
899176400

Shape spotting
How to play:
Go for a walk (outdoors or around your house). How many rectangles can you find?&nbsp;
Remember: a square is a special kind of rectangle, so you can include squares in your count.&nbsp;
Here are some examples:

Spells
A koala dressed as a wizardHow to play:
Invent your own spell words and teach them to the other person. Do they put the stressed syllables in the correct place?
When you are a witch or a wizard, you will need to say lots of spells. But the way you say the word will affect whether the spell works.
For example:&nbsp;
wingardium leviosa ✔&nbsp;
wingardium leviosa ✘&nbsp;

Who's most like...
A Koala thinking about Mathilda.How to play:
Pick a character (or roll the dice below - roll again if you get one you don't know).&nbsp;
Both players must think of another character they know (book, tv, film etc) who is most similar.&nbsp;
Spend a moment thinking through your reasons.&nbsp;
Share your reasons and decide whose character is most similar.&nbsp;
Repeat with a new character.

The hat game
A hat lying upside-down on the floor, with folded scraps of paper inside it.You will need:
A hat, bowl, or other container to put your words in;
A piece of paper torn or cut into pieces to write words on (it can be scrap paper, e.g. part of an old envelope);
A pen or pencil;
A one minute timer (you can use the one at the bottom of this page).&nbsp;
How to play:
Player 1 has one minute to describe as many of the words from the container as they can for Player 2 to guess.
Player 1 should keep any words that are guessed. Return any un-guessed words to the container.
When the time runs out, swap over. Now Player 2 has one minute to describe as many of the words from the container as they can for Player 2 to guess.&nbsp;
Player 2 should keep any words that are guessed. Return any un-guessed words to the container.&nbsp;
Keep taking it in turns to describe words until there are no words left.
The winner is the player who described the most words!&nbsp;
Getting ready:
Use twelve scraps of paper each. Both players should write:
The names of four famous people
Four other {{definition "nouns" "Nouns are things like objects, e.g. 'horse', 'apple' or 'love'."}}
Four {{definition "verbs" "Verbs are doing words, e.g. 'running' or 'flying'."}}
Fold all the pieces of paper in half, and put them in your container.

Magic sounds box
How does this help?How to play:
Sit opposite your child, holding the box.&nbsp;
The box becomes ‘magic’ when you turn it around in your hand, saying the rhyme:
Turn, turn, turn the box round,
Lift the lid and listen for the sound!
Point to your ear as you say ‘listen’.&nbsp;
As you open the lid, make a sound with your voice that matches one of the objects.  Keep the sound going (with the lid in front of our mouth) until you shut the lid, to extend the magic!&nbsp;
Can your child guess the sound? Can they make the sounds with you?&nbsp;
You will need:
A small box with a lid (ideally a flap lid, e.g. teabag or laundry powder tablet boxes are good). You can decorate the box if you are feeling creative!
Small objects or pictures of things that would make a sound (e.g. toy animals or vehicles) to put inside the box.
A box decorated with colourful wrapping paper. The lid is flapped open and a toy duck is peeping out. It has a label on the fron that reads "Magic Sounds: Turn, tur, turn the box round, Lift the lif and listen for the sound!"

Stories and rhymes
Stories and routines can be a useful and enjoyable part of routines. They help to strengthen the bond between you and your child, while learning and having fun!  &nbsp;
A grandma and a toddler looking at a book together.

Stories and rhymes
Here are some nature themed ideas for stories and rhymes:&nbsp;
A mum and a baby looking at a book together outdoors.

One, two buckle my shoe
A pair of buckled shoesHere are the song words:
One, two, buckle my shoe;
Three, four, knock at the door;
Five, six, pick up sticks;
Seven, eight, lay them straight;
Nine, ten, a big fat hen;
Eleven, twelve, dig and delve;
Thirteen, fourteen, maids a-courting;
Fifteen, sixteen, maids in the kitchen;
Seventeen, eighteen, maids in waiting;
Nineteen, twenty, my plate's empty.
In this song, each line has two numbers.&nbsp;
The first is odd.&nbsp;
The even number is part of the rhyme.&nbsp;
Can you spot the even numbers and the words they rhyme with?

Finding paragraphs
What to do:
Lots of different kinds of writing includes paragraphs.&nbsp;
How many can you think of?&nbsp;
Can you find any examples of different kinds of writing that use paragraphs?
Paragraphs in a newspaper, a book and board game instructions.

